From 1796b4ebdc4c0e537cdc8842602cfcd5ad108fa7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 21:42:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(camera): render core-resolved server camera movement

Subscribe to ViaBedrock's versioned camera fade channel on direct and
ViaProxy connections. Draw the supplied color and opacity timeline while
preserving the HUD, including when the normal HUD is hidden. Render the
remaining elapsed state after late registration instead of restarting it.

Fade merging and protocol decoding belong to core. The add-on only owns
the client clock and overlay extraction, and releases state at disconnect.
Splines and aim assistance remain separate requirements. Native screenshot comparison must establish overlay order.

Apply server FOV to the world camera before projection and culling setup.
Use the shared core transition rules with the previous rendered projection
and the unmodified local projection, preserving native overshoot and clear
lifecycle. Queue commands until a projection context exists and restore
elapsed snapshots without replaying the effect from its beginning. Keep
the separate first-person hand projection and release state at disconnect.

Subscribe to the shared camera shake state. Apply world-space position and
pitch/yaw offsets after ordinary camera alignment and before culling.
Keep entity aim and outbound movement unchanged. Add the native Allow
Camera Shake preference, ignore new shakes while disabled, consume stop,
and release all state on disconnect. Core owns queues and noise sampling.

Apply core-resolved free-camera targets with the shared native blend
updater. Supply the previous rendered frame on interrupted transitions,
apply the pose before shake and culling, and select server perspectives
without changing the saved user view. Clear and disconnect release the
override. Ordinary player movement and outbound aim remain unchanged.

Restore settled targets for late subscriptions instead of synthesizing a
new transition from an unknown old frame. Built-in first/third-person
alignment currently uses Java's camera collision and distance rules, so
activation works but those poses do not establish Bedrock camera parity.
Audio/effects, follow cameras, splines and attached targets remain gaps.
